1.1.text:
Article 3

1. The objectives of implementing protection for women and children are to: a. prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children; b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children; d. provide services to women and children, reporters, and witnesses;

Perda Kab. HSU Tahun 2016 Nomor 3 ttg Perlindungan Perempuan dan Anak
e. empower women for the fulfillment of the rights of women and children; andf. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize harmonious and prosperous household integrity.
2. The objectives of implementing protection for women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:a. prevention;
b. service and assistance;
c. reunification;
d. empowerment.

Article 16

1. To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows:a. increasing the quantity and quality of education, both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. imposing curfew restrictions for children (under 18 years of age) leaving home at night no later than 11.00 p.m., if beyond that time they must be accompanied by parents or family;
g. restricting children's access to the internet at internet cafes (warnet) during school hours;
h. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; andi. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

CHAPTER V

DUTIES AND AUTHORITIES OF THE REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 10

1. The Regional Government has the duty to carry out efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children, in the form of:a. collecting data and information about women and children victims of acts of violence, and laws and regulations;
b. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection of women and children;
c. conducting education on anti-violence values against women and children; andd. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection of women and children.
2. The Regional Government in anticipating the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, has the authority to provide and organize services for victims in the form of:a. providing and facilitating the establishment of integrated services for victims of acts of violence by involving institutions and community elements;
b. encouraging community awareness of the importance of protection for victims; andc. conducting community empowerment for the prevention of acts of violence.

Article 24

1. In order to achieve the objectives of providing protection to women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government shall cooperate with:a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. universities; ande. non-governmental institutions.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), shall include: a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; andd. provision of evidence and witnesses, and followed up in accordance with applicable law.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), shall be set forth in the form of a joint agreement.

Article 17

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 16, shall be implemented by SKPDs whose duties and functions are in the fields of: Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Childrena. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; andg. public order and security.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by SKPDs as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 22

1. The Regional Government is obligated to conduct empowerment of women victims of acts of violence.
2. Empowerment of victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented by P2TP2A.
3. In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall formulate empowerment programs in the economic field, which include:a. procuring the needs required for entrepreneurship training for women, in order to increase business knowledge, attitudes, and skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop women's businesses;
e. endeavoring to provide capital for women victims of acts of violence; and Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Childrenf. expanding access to information and promoting women's products.
4. In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 20

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence, are as follows:a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; andg. psychological services.
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Government and implemented by SKPDs whose duties and functions are in the fields of: Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Childrena. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and d. mental and spiritual.
3. In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the Regional Government shall cooperate with Government agencies, Provincial Government, other Regency/City Governments, and the community.
4. Further provisions regarding procedures for services and handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER XII

INVESTIGATION

Article 33

1. In addition to general investigation officers, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation, Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Children may also be carried out by Civil Servant Investigators (PPNS) as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Law.
2. PPNS as referred to in paragraph (1), are certain civil servant officials within the Regional Government who are appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. In carrying out investigation duties, PPNS as referred to in paragraph (2), are authorized to: a. receive reports or complaints from someone about the existence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take first action at that time at the scene and conduct examination;
c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identification;
d. seize objects and/or documents;
e. take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in experts needed in connection with case examination;
h. stop the investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident is not a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the Public Prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
4. PPNS as referred to in paragraph (2), notify the commencement of investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the Public Prosecutor through the Police Investigator Officer of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Law.

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 6 letter a, include: Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Childrena. implementing policies for the protection of women and children established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children in accordance with regional financial capacity;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for implementing protection of women and children.
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 14

Forms of services to victims organized by P2TP2A include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services are a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services are services provided by companions in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength and independence in resolving their problems;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;
e. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and can provide economic access so that victims can be independent.

