1. To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows:a. increasing the quantity and quality of education, both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. imposing curfew restrictions for children (under 18 years of age) leaving home at night no later than 11.00 p.m., if beyond that time they must be accompanied by parents or family;
g. restricting children's access to the internet at internet cafes (warnet) during school hours;
h. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; andi. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 22

1. The Regional Government is obligated to conduct empowerment of women victims of acts of violence.
2. Empowerment of victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented by P2TP2A.
3. In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall formulate empowerment programs in the economic field, which include:a. procuring the needs required for entrepreneurship training for women, in order to increase business knowledge, attitudes, and skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop women's businesses;
e. endeavoring to provide capital for women victims of acts of violence; and Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Childrenf. expanding access to information and promoting women's products.
4. In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 6 letter a, include: Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Childrena. implementing policies for the protection of women and children established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children in accordance with regional financial capacity;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for implementing protection of women and children.
